98
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:43,000
of the Colorado experiment.

99
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:45,000
Those of you that have been following along

100
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:49,000
will recall that we're tracking the progress

101
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:51,000
of a brand new troop out in Colorado.

102
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:53,000
We got Arlam Ward, the brand new scout master

103
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:56,000
of that brand new troop, to give us an update

104
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:58,000
And that's going to take up the rest of the podcast.

105
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:00,000
So let's get started, shall we?

106
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:09,000
And now our next episode of the Colorado experiment.

107
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:21,000
Well, I'm happy to welcome Arlam Ward

108
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:23,000
back to the Scout Master Podcast.

109
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:27,000
We're checking up on troop 17 out there in Colorado.

110
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:28,000
It's a brand new troop

111
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:31,000
And I think, Arlam, this is our third installment, right?

112
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:32,000
Yes,

113
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:33,000
Just to catch everybody up.

114
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:35,000
Previously on the Colorado experiment,

115
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:37,000
Right? previously on the Colorado experiment,

116
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:38,000
That's what I was looking for.

117
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,000
So previously on the Colorado experiment,

118
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:43,000
we kind of tracked the whole idea of starting a troop.

119
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:45,000
Let's just go back there real quick

120
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:47,000
in case somebody hasn't heard the whole story.

121
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:49,000
You decided to go ahead and start a scout troop.

122
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:51,000
You had been a cub master

123
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:55,000
And you found that there were any number of boys

124
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:59,000
in your locality who had one time been in scouts

125
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:03,000
who decided that the idea of a new troop was appealing to them

126
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:06,000
and they decided to come back in.

127
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:08,000
It was originally started as a conversation

128
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:12,000
between families that had weeblos

129
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:15,000
and we were starting to look around at troops

130
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:17,000
and the conversations were:

131
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:19,000
well, we can do this or we can do this,

132
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:21,000
or we can start one.

133
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:23,000
Yeah, don't have those conversations, folks,

134
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:24,000
You're going to be right,

135
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:28,000
Otherwise you'll be on the podcast as an experiment.

136
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:33,000
That's where it started.

137
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:37,000
And then, talking to the charter partner,

138
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:40,000
it was: well, there's a number of boys

139
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:43,000
that have dropped out of scouting. that like to.

140
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:45,000
The part that really resonated with me

141
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:46,000
is that they like to go camping,

142
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:48,000
They like to do the outdoor stuff,

143
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:49,000
but they dropped out of scouting

144
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:51,000
And that was just kind of well. okay,

145
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:53,000
that seems to me that there's a different approach

146
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:55,000
that we can take to the scouting

147
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:57,000
that will keep those boys involved.

148
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:59,000
The last time we spoke

149
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:03,000
you were still waiting on having, like, a meeting place.

150
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:04,000
Have you got a meeting place, yet

151
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:06,000
Temporarily we have one.

152
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:07,000
There's a church in our neighborhood

153
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:09,000
that makes their facility available,

154
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:11,000
So we have set up through the summer

155
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:12,000
to work with this church

156
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:14,000
before we move over to our permanent home.

157
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:16,000
I believe the last time we talked

158
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:18,000
you had been out on a hike

159
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:21,000
but you were looking forward to your first camping trip.

160
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:23,000
Tell us a little bit about how that came about

161
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:24,000
and how it went.

162
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:26,000
Okay, when we sat down originally

163
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:30,000
with the very initial parts of the PLC

164
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:33,000
and said, okay, let's start naming activities

165
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:34,000
that we want to do on a monthly basis,

166
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:35,000
We want to do something this month

167
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:37,000
but we don't have time to really put much together,

168
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:38,000
So let's go hiking.

169
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:40,000
Then we set a date for the first camping trip,

170
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:42,000
which was over Memorial Day weekend.

171
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:44,000
So we weren't really planning on doing a summer camp.

172
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:45,000
but the boys came back and said:

173
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:47,000
you know, we would like to go to summer camp.

174
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:49,000
So we found a summer camp to go to

175
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:51,000
and set that up for July.

176
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:52,000
And then in August

177
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:55,000
we have camping trips specific to the PLC,

178
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:57,000
doing annual planning and leadership training

179
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:58,000
and stuff like that,

180
00:08:58,000 --> 00:09:00,000
Then kicking off the school year

181
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:03,000
with a trained PLC

182
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:07,000
and probably a court of honor and stuff like that.

183
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:08,000
And then after that

184
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:11,000
it all depends on what they do in August.

185
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,000
And so how did this camping trip go

186
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:15,000
that you did on Memorial Day weekend?

187
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:18,000
Nothing ever goes quite as planned,

188
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:19,000
Get out,

189
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:20,000
Really.

190
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:24,000
So the week that we were supposed to go camping,

191
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:26,000
I get a call from the camp post

192
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:29,000
in the National Forest where we were going.

193
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:30,000
He called me.

194
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:31,000
He's like: yeah,

195
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:33,000
you have a reservation for this coming weekend.

196
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:36,000
There's still two feet of snow in your campsite.

197
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:39,000
So we told him we'd find something else

198
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:41,000
And we moved the campsite.

199
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:43,000
but it turns out the place we were at was fantastic.

200
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:44,000
As the adults

201
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:47,000
we didn't really set much in the way of agenda.

202
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:48,000
The boys,

203
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:51,000
when they had set up this initial camping trip,

204
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,000
said: let's do something where we all get together

205
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:54,000
and get to know each other.

206
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:57,000
He goes because we don't all really know each other at this point.

207
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:01,000
And there was about 10 scouts that went on. this trip

208
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:03,000
Had a lot of parents.

209
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:05,000
I was really surprised at the number of parents that went

210
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:08,000
And I think they were just kind of curious to see how this was going to go.

211
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:11,000
So we had almost as many parents as we had scouts.

212
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:14,000
The scouts were separated from each other.

213
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:17,000
The two patrols were separated from each other by about 100 yards

214
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:21,000
And they were separated from us by about 100 or 150 yards as well.

215
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:24,000
And I kept all the parents up with me

216
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:26,000
And when we had talked beforehand

217
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:28,000
I said: bring a chair- preferably a good book-

218
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:30,000
and stay out of their campsites.

219
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:34,000
It was where we started that conversation

220
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:38,000
And it worked out really well,

221
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,000
A lot better than I expected.

222
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:43,000
We had two new patrol leaders,

223
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:46,000
One's 12 and one's 13..

224
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,000
They'd never been patrol leaders before.

225
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:49,000
They were very excited about it.

226
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,000
They set up their own campsites.

227
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,000
We kind of gave them an area and you know

228
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:54,000
beforehand had mentioned to them.

229
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:57,000
perhaps you want to read through your book where it says how to pick a campsite,

230
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,000
because you're going to be picking one.

231
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:04,000
One patrol brought a gigantic eight-person tent

232
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:07,000
and put everybody in one tent,

233
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:10,000
which was interesting.

234
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:13,000
I'm not sure they'll do that again.

235
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:16,000
It was an experiment.

236
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:18,000
They did their own cooking,

237
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:19,000
They did their own breakfast.

238
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
We got together for dinners,

239
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:24,000
But it was a good chance for us to sit around and eat together

240
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:26,000
and talk and things like that.

241
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:31,000
Most of my weekend was checking in everyone somewhere with the patrol leaders

242
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:33,000
and seeing what they were up to.

243
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:36,000
We figured, since we were coming up with Dutch ovens and cars

244
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:37,000
and things like that,

245
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:40,000
it was a good time to go through all the ax yard stuff,

246
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:43,000
since you don't want to take those on a backpacking trip.

247
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
The patrols would rotate through

248
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:48,000
and they'd talk to one of the adults over at the ax yard

249
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:51,000
who was teaching them about what an ax yard was

250
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:52,000
and how it works,

251
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,000
and somebody else went through how to sharpen an ax.

252
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:56,000
For the rest of the weekend

253
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:58,000
they chopped wood for fun,

254
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:01,000
which it was completely unexpected on my part.

255
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:05,000
The adults were laughing.

256
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:06,000
If we had sat down and said:

257
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:10,000
here's a half of a pickup truck full of wood.

258
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
your job for the rest of the weekend

259
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
is to split it into smaller pieces

260
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
and burn it all up before we go home.

261
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:19,000
It would have been complaints and drudgery and things like that.

262
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:21,000
But since it was their idea that they want-

263
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,000
hey, we want to have another fire,

264
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,000
Can we chop more wood?

265
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:29,000
For the most part it was all kind of their direction.

266
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:32,000
The patrol leader stepped up and did a fantastic job,

267
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:34,000
better than I had ever hoped.

268
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:37,000
One patrol was up before the adults.

269
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
They had cooked their breakfast

270
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
and had cleaned everything up

271
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:43,000
and were ready to go fishing

272
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:46,000
before the adults were even done with their breakfast.

273
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,000
They're definitely motivated.

274
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:50,000
It resonates with them

275
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:52,000
The whole working as a patrol thing.

276
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:55,000
We did the start, stop and continue stuff

277
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:58,000
as a whole group during dinner the last night

278
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,000
And then I also met with the patrol leaders separately.

279
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,000
The senior patrol leaders didn't show up,

280
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:03,000
both being in high school

281
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:06,000
and it being the weekend, right after school got out.

282
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:08,000
They were otherwise engaged,

283
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:11,000
So it was really the two patrol leaders.

284
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:14,000
It was very eye-opening for them

285
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:17,000
and very eye-opening for me as well.

286
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:19,000
I had a patrol leader that said

287
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:21,000
I had a hard time this weekend getting people

288
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:24,000
to do the jobs that they were assigned to do.

289
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:26,000
When I was meeting with the two patrol leaders

290
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:28,000
we kind of talked about that for a little bit.

291
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:31,000
I said: well, a lot of times

292
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:34,000
it's one of those things where,

293
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:37,000
as a patrol leader and the older guys,

294
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,000
you guys want to do the fun stuff,

295
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:42,000
So you assign each other to do the fun parts,

296
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:43,000
the cooking parts,

297
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:46,000
and then leave the younger, less experienced guys

298
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:48,000
to do the cleanup.

299
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:50,000
I said it's much more effective, first of all,

300
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:52,000
to put an experienced guy with a young guy

301
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,000
so that you at least have somebody involved

302
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
that knows what's going on.

303
00:13:56,000 --> 00:14:00,000
Another thing is to write it out as a duty roster.

304
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:02,000
I had mentioned it beforehand,

305
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:03,000
but they didn't do it,

306
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,000
And so we talked about it afterwards and said,

307
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:07,000
well, this is one of the reasons, so that everybody can see

308
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:10,000
that the load is being shared around,

309
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:11,000
that it's not favoritism,

310
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:12,000
You're not picking on anybody.

311
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:14,000
But here, written in black and white,

312
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:17,000
is: the duty is to get us through the weekend as a patrol,

313
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
and that that's really useful.

314
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:20,000
And he took it to heart.

315
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:22,000
The other patrol leader had a suggestion as well.

316
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:25,000
He goes: well, what we did was we had specific people cook,

317
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:27,000
but then everybody was responsible for cleaning up.

318
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:29,000
It's those little challenges along the way

319
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
and the discussions that we have

320
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:33,000
as the patrol leaders counsel.

321
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:37,000
that kind of moves them towards more proficiency.

322
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,000
You made a suggestion.

323
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:41,000
You kind of pointed out: okay,

324
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
this might be a good idea, fellas,

325
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:46,000
And both the patrols basically ignored you,

326
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:49,000
Not out of any ill will or anything like that.

327
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
They just, yeah, well, Mr Ward,

328
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:52,000
that's a great idea.

329
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:53,000
Thanks,

330
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
They were focused on other things. yes,

331
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
Yes, they got to experiment

332
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,000
and they discovered that, jeez,

333
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,000
the old man might know something.

334
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:03,000
We might actually give that a try

335
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:06,000
To me that kind of defines the scout approach.

336
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:08,000
Instead of castigating them and saying, jeez,

337
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:10,000
well, you know, if you've just done what I told you,

338
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:13,000
you kind of go now. do you see the reason behind this?

339
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:16,000
Do you get the idea that this might be a good idea?

340
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:19,000
It sounds like you may have won one convert

341
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:22,000
and one had a way of just doing it by himself.

342
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:25,000
Yeah, which work this time, may not work next time,

343
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
or it may work forever,

344
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,000
and then he never actually uses a duty roster.

345
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
you know, that's fine with me as long as it

346
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
patrol functions. but when it comes to me later

347
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:35,000
and says: you know, I still can't get everybody

348
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:37,000
to do their jobs, I suppose

349
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
we'll talk about it again.

350
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:42,000
That's the heart of letting boys discover

351
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:44,000
what they need to discover.

352
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:46,000
You've cultivated a bit of a tolerance

353
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:48,000
for the adversity and uncertainty

354
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:50,000
that that type of thing causes.

355
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:53,000
Did you get any kind of resistance

356
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:55,000
from the other adults involved,

357
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:58,000
maybe being less tolerant about that idea?

358
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:00,000
Oh, absolutely.

359
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:04,000
Actually, on that very same topic,

360
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:06,000
I was talking to one of the

361
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,000
assistant scout masters and he said

362
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:10,000
I had noticed that they didn't do a duty roster

363
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:12,000
and I was going to go tell them they had to

364
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,000
and we talked and said, no, it's better this way,

365
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:16,000
that they tried their method.

366
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
they didn't seem to work quite as well,

367
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
so it bears a lot more weight. you know,

368
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,000
when they can align it with their own experience

369
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:24,000
rather than great. here comes Mr Ward.

370
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:26,000
he's got all these forms I have to fill out

371
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:28,000
for no reason, when really all we want to do

372
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:30,000
is go to the X-Arch and chop wood.

373
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:32,000
it's so much better when it comes back from them.

374
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:34,000
you know, sometimes they spend an

375
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:36,000
inordinate amount of time actually cleaning

376
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:38,000
after a meal,

377
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:42,000
which I understand isn't

378
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:44,000
Not unusual-

379
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
Yeah, it's not unique to my troop,

380
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,000
but they got better at it as the weekend went on.

381
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
but I had one patrol the first

382
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,000
day that I think breakfast

383
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:54,000
kind of spilled over into lunch

384
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:56,000
but nobody was leaving the adult area

385
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,000
and running down there to see what the problem was.

386
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:00,000
No, no, there was concerns.

387
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
you know, parents going,

388
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,000
what are the patrols doing?

389
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
and they said, well, you know this one's doing this one,

390
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,000
this one's, you know, finishing up breakfast

391
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:10,000
and they're like: still, it's 10.30, it's 11 o'clock.

392
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
it's like, well, yes,

393
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:14,000
but they're working out.

394
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,000
you know best way to clean their stuff.

395
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:18,000
and I talked to a number of adults

396
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
over the weekend, you know,

397
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:22,000
especially having a lot of the parents there,

398
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,000
I think they saw the benefit.

399
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:26,000
a number of them came up to me

400
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:28,000
as the weekend went on and said

401
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,000
I understand a lot more about scouting now

402
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,000
than I did before this weekend started.

403
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:34,000
you know it's like you put them down there

404
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
and they have to figure it out on their own.

405
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,000
you know like it was,

406
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
like it was my idea,

407
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,000
Brilliant, it was absolutely brilliant.

408
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
Yeah, it was like.

409
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,000
yeah, well, that's true.

410
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:48,000
you know we need to keep an eye out

411
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:50,000
for certain things,

412
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,000
like the safety things,

413
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,000
and you know the overwhelming frustration

414
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:56,000
that you know can set in after a while.

415
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,000
you know where you give them

416
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,000
a bit of direction to go along with that,

417
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
but for the most part you leave it to them

418
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:04,000
to settle it out themselves.

419
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:06,000
there's a couple of times I had to

420
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:08,000
kind of follow a parent that I saw

421
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,000
wandering off towards the patrol areas

422
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,000
and I'd follow, kind of follow them down there

423
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
and I'd say, you know they'd get down there

424
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
and they'd ask the boys how things were going

425
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:18,000
and then when they'd start reaching for stuff,

426
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:20,000
come back with me, let's go this way,

427
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:22,000
and they'd look at me like they got caught,

428
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
and they'd kind of hit me in the head

429
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
and come back up

430
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,000
and come back up, but in talking to some of the

431
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:30,000
scouts afterwards, I didn't

432
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,000
catch as much of that as I think

433
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,000
I probably should have.

434
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,000
for the most part, though, you know, let them

435
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:38,000
figure things out.

436
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:40,000
it's not like we kept them segregated all weekend long.

437
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,000
when the patrol says, hey, I want to go fishing,

438
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,000
well, you know the dad's from that patrol-

439
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
most of them went with them.

440
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:48,000
they should be able to kind of suss this out a little bit,

441
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
and they should be able to turn to the adults

442
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,000
and say: thank you very much,

443
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
but we're good,

444
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
go away now, leave us alone.

445
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
that's a hard thing

446
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,000
for boys to be able to do, though,

447
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
you know. I think a lot of that comes

448
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,000
from something that

449
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,000
doesn't get stressed enough.

450
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
you know, praise them for the littlest things,

451
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
and I know that you've talked about this before,

452
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,000
and you know, in trainings and things like that,

453
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
it doesn't come up nearly as often as it should,

454
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,000
which is

455
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
when they do the littlest things right.

456
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
keep on the praise,

457
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
reframe the failures,

458
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,000
as we could do this better.

459
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,000
we- you know, not you,

460
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
but we could do this better,

461
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,000
even just in the course of a weekend

462
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
you see the difference in their confidence.

463
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
so you talk to them about: hey, you know

464
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
you guys are doing great, but there's a couple of things we could improve.

465
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
when we did the stop, start and continue,

466
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
it was the same thing. you know,

467
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
you guys had a great weekend, we had a lot of fun.

468
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
but let's just kind of take a minute to review.

469
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,000
so you're talking about a process

470
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
called stop, start and continue.

471
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
just tell me what the basics

472
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,000
of that process are.

473
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,000
it's a review tool. you know. stop,

474
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
it's the things that you're going to stop doing,

475
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
things that didn't work out for us,

476
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
things that cause problems for us.

477
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
these are the things that we're going to stop doing.

478
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
but you don't run down there and say stop,

479
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,000
stop, don't do that.

480
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,000
this is reflective.

481
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
yeah, this is a reflection tool. for sure.

482
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
it's definitely better if these things come from the scouts

483
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
than if they come from the adults.

484
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
I was kind of leading them through the process.

485
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,000
the adults were quiet the whole time.

486
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,000
these conversations came from the scouts,

487
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
which was a bit surprising,

488
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
but of course, at this point

489
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
they had been through two days and we tell them-

490
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:28,000
and believe the boys alone-

491
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
maybe they were listening to them,

492
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
chasing them down, throwing a lasso around.

493
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
yeah

494
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,000
right, gentle, redirection.

495
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:38,000
so there's the things we're going to stop doing,

496
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,000
the things that we should continue doing,

497
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:42,000
and I think that's great that it ends with the continue stuff.

498
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:44,000
when we started that conversation,

499
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:46,000
you know I said this is a review tool.

500
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:48,000
what sort of things have come up

501
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
over the weekend that were issues?

502
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:52,000
and then a patrol leader raised his hand,

503
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:54,000
he goes. you know, we need to work harder

504
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:56,000
at including everybody.

505
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
that was kind of open the floodgate

506
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:00,000
to that point. so you know we should work harder

507
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:02,000
at helping people

508
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,000
feel included, that even if,

509
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:06,000
as we go along, you guys are going to be,

510
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,000
you know, tighten it as a patrol,

511
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:10,000
but there's going to be outsiders

512
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:12,000
of visitors that are going to be joining you

513
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,000
and you're going to have to spend some time

514
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:16,000
making sure that they're included

515
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:18,000
and keep that in mind as you're going along.

516
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,000
and that just led into a whole bunch of other things

517
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,000
that they discussed

518
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,000
as far as things that we should start doing

519
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:26,000
or stop doing.

520
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,000
so this isn't your opportunity as the scout master

521
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:30,000
to lay down a lecture.

522
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:32,000
